## About this pack

This pack proposes a new incident categorisation model for LIMSOC, Life Saving Victoria's operational incident-management system, and maps every current incident type to the new model. Nothing in this pack is endorsed yet.

**How to read it.** Part 1 explains the problem we have today. Part 2 explains the proposed model, one concept at a time, and why each piece exists. Part 3 describes what will change for operators, duty officers, and our leadership. Part 4 covers what happens next and what we need from you. The annexes hold the working detail: the full migration map for all 72 current types, the decision log, and the platform notes.

Feedback to Nicholas Wood, State Lifesaving Communications Officer.

---

# Part 1: The case for change

## The problem

The current model provides no written definition of what an incident type represents or when it should be updated. In the absence of a definition, the type has been asked to carry every dimension of an incident: what happened, how serious it was, who was involved, how it ended, and who requested our attendance. A field that carries all of these dimensions cannot classify any of them reliably, and each operator's interpretation of it necessarily differs.

As a result, a single incident can reasonably be categorised in several different ways, and in current practice it is:

- There is no guidance on when something is a Marine Incident versus a Vessel Incident, so the same capsize is recorded differently on different shifts.
- With no better guidance, a large share of in-water work is generalised to Rescue (In Water), Condition Unknown, and the detail is lost.
- Recovered from Water and Resuscitation overlap with no rule between them. Today we cannot tell whether a resuscitation was a patient encountered on the beach or a person retrieved from the water.
- We cannot distinguish between assisting another agency to do their job and delivering our own service with other agencies alongside. Both look the same in the data.
- There is no rule for when an incident type should be updated, so update practice varies with each individual's experience and view of what should happen. When a rescue becomes a drowning, the type is frequently overwritten and the rescue is lost from the record. Major Rescues cannot be reliably searched, because some are no longer typed as rescues.
- The same confusion exists between records: there is no clear rule for when something is an incident, a rescue log, or a first aid form, so it is done inconsistently, and our statistics inherit that inconsistency.

These problems share a single root cause: no field in the current model carries a written definition, so practice on each has drifted into individual interpretation.

**The cost.** Every season, the EMCaR team pulls incident data for SLSA, for councils, for LSV, and for our own clubs and services. Because the data cannot be taken at face value, each pull requires manual interrogation and correction before the figures can be relied upon. The effort is substantial, it recurs every season, and it limits what can be delivered: reporting that cannot be produced, trends that cannot be seen, and questions that cannot be answered.

## Why now

The need to redesign incident categorisation has been recognised for years. It has not been done because the problem is genuinely complex: 72 types, years of accumulated practice, and interagency dependencies. The level of detail in this pack reflects that complexity. The underlying solution, however, is simple, and every element of the design follows from a single principle set out in Part 2.

Two developments make this the right time.

**The role has changed.** LSV Comms once spent much of its effort signing on every unit. Digital advances in LIMSOC have removed that workload. The principal responsibility of LSV Comms is now data quality: capturing what happened in an incident accurately and consistently. This proposal is a direct exercise of that responsibility.

**The approach is proven.** Last season the first aid form was rebuilt from a rudimentary form that was not fit for purpose. The previous form asked a small set of fixed questions that frequently did not match the situation and gave members no way to record the details that mattered. The replacement asks questions based on what occurred: where oxygen use is recorded, the oxygen questions are presented. The result was visibility that had not existed before. For the first time the data could be interrogated with confidence: which first aid presentations are trending, where forms are completed incorrectly, and what is occurring on our beaches. This proposal applies the same transformation to the incident record.

The risk profile of the change should also be stated clearly. The current structure is already producing inconsistent data, and every season it operates adds to that inconsistency. The transition carries effort, but it carries no realistic risk of degrading data quality below its current state. It can only improve it.

